Here's the lesson program making the fineness of the box topology — and the failure of finite subcovers — visible and draggable.

```typescript
// The essence: box-open sets pin EVERY coordinate at once, so they are tiny,
// so an open cover made of such boxes admits no finite subcover.
// One parameter drives the whole story: how many coordinates a box constrains.

const topology = M.parameter("topology (0 = product, 1 = box)", 0, { range: [0, 1] });
// In product topology a basic open constrains only finitely many coords;
// in box topology it constrains ALL of them.
const constrained = M.derived(
  () => (topology.value >= 0.5 ? Infinity : 3),
  "coordinates a basic open constrains",
);

// How many boxes we are allowed to keep in a finite subfamily.
const finiteBudget = M.parameter("boxes kept in the subfamily", 2, { range: [0, 8] });

// ── Beat 1 ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
lesson.beat("Stack the coordinates forever");
const space = View.diagram(
  { domain: [0, 1], range: [0, 1], cols: 8 },
  (pen) => {
    pen.bands(8, { recede: 0.84 }).forEach((c, k) => {
      pen.band(c.center, { width: c.width, role: "free" });
      // each slot carries a 0/1 coordinate value
      pen.text(c.center, 0.62, k % 2 === 0 ? "0" : "1", { size: 26, role: "neutral" });
      pen.text(c.center, 0.28, `x_{${k}}`, { size: 18, role: "neutral" });
    });
    pen.text(0.5, 0.9, "\\cdots", { size: 30, role: "neutral" });
  },
  { label: "\\{0,1\\}^{\\mathbb{N}} \\;=\\; \\prod_{i} \\{0,1\\}" },
);
lesson.show(space);
lesson.say`A point of ${space} is an infinite sequence of 0s and 1s — one choice in each of infinitely many slots, receding forever to the right.`;

// ── Beat 2 ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
lesson.beat("Open boxes down every slot");
const boxDef = F.tex`${F.span("box", "\\prod_i U_i")} \quad\text{with } U_i \subseteq \{0,1\} \text{ open}`;
lesson.show(View.formula(boxDef));
// A diagram whose constrained-count is LIVE: crank topology and the pinned
// slots spread from "finitely many" to "all of them".
const basicOpen = View.diagram(
  { domain: [0, 1], range: [0, 1], cols: 8 },
  (pen) => {
    const k = constrained.value; // Infinity in box topology
    pen.bands(8, { recede: 0.84 }).forEach((c, j) => {
      const pinned = j < k; // in box topology, every slot is pinned
      pen.band(c.center, { width: c.width, role: pinned ? "constraint" : "free" });
      pen.marker(c.center, 0.6, { state: pinned ? "constrained" : "free" });
    });
    pen.text(0.5, 0.9, Number.isFinite(k) ? `${k} slots pinned` : "every slot pinned", {
      size: 22,
      role: Number.isFinite(k) ? "neutral" : "reject",
    });
  },
  { label: "a basic open set" },
);
lesson.show(basicOpen);
lesson.say`In the ${boxDef} the product topology only lets ${constrained} finitely many slots be pinned. The box topology pins them ALL at once. Push ${topology} to see the constraint spread down every slot.`;
lesson.vary(topology, { to: 1, over: "2s" });

// ── Beat 3 ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
lesson.beat("Boxes can be tiny everywhere");
const isFine = M.claim({
  predicate: () => !Number.isFinite(constrained.value),
  label: "a basic open can isolate a point (all coordinates fixed)",
});
lesson.show(View.claim(isFine));
lesson.say`Because ${constrained} is infinite in the box topology, a single box like ${basicOpen} can fix every coordinate — the neighborhood of a point can be almost the point itself. The topology is very fine, and points get nearly isolated: ${isFine} now holds.`;
lesson.focus(basicOpen, { emphasize: "stillness" });

// ── Beat 4 ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
lesson.beat("Cover with point-pinning boxes");
// A cover: box n pins coordinate n to force it, so box n captures only a thin
// slice. We draw several boxes as rows, each pinning many slots.
const cover = View.diagram(
  { domain: [0, 1], range: [0, 1], cols: 8 },
  (pen) => {
    const bands = pen.bands(8, { recede: 0.84 });
    const rows = pen.bands(5, { padding: 0.08 }); // 5 boxes of the cover, stacked
    rows.forEach((r, n) => {
      bands.forEach((c, j) => {
        // each box n pins infinitely many coordinates (drawn: nearly all)
        const pinned = j !== n % 8;
        pen.band(c.center, {
          width: c.width * 0.7,
          y0: r.lo,
          y1: r.hi,
          role: pinned ? "constraint" : "escape",
        });
      });
      pen.text(0.02, r.center, `B_{${n}}`, { size: 16, role: "neutral", anchor: "start" });
    });
  },
  { label: "\\mathcal{U} = \\{B_0, B_1, B_2, \\dots\\}" },
);
lesson.show(cover);
lesson.say`Build a cover ${cover}: each box pins infinitely many coordinates, leaving only one slot free (shown green). So each box grabs only a thin slice of the whole product — yet together they reach every point.`;

// ── Beat 5 ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
lesson.beat("Finitely many can't fill it");
// Keep only finitely many boxes; a point can be built to disagree with every
// one of them on the slot each leaves free. That escaping point exists exactly
// when the budget is finite (always, here) — the claim never breaks.
const escapes = M.claim({
  predicate: () => finiteBudget.value < 8, // any finite choice misses a point
  label: "a point escapes every box in the finite subfamily",
});
lesson.show(View.claim(escapes));
const subfamily = View.diagram(
  { domain: [0, 1], range: [0, 1], cols: 8 },
  (pen) => {
    const kept = Math.round(finiteBudget.value);
    const bands = pen.bands(8, { recede: 0.84 });
    const rows = pen.bands(5, { padding: 0.08 });
    rows.forEach((r, n) => {
      const inSub = n < kept;
      bands.forEach((c, j) => {
        const pinned = j !== n % 8;
        pen.band(c.center, {
          width: c.width * 0.7,
          y0: r.lo,
          y1: r.hi,
          role: inSub ? (pinned ? "constraint" : "escape") : "free",
          fill: inSub,
        });
      });
      pen.text(0.02, r.center, inSub ? `B_{${n}}` : "\\cdot", {
        size: 16,
        role: inSub ? "neutral" : "neutral",
        anchor: "start",
      });
    });
    // the escaping point: pick, in each freed slot, the value the box forbids
    pen.text(0.5, 0.94, "escaping point p", { size: 18, role: "reject" });
    bands.forEach((c) => pen.marker(c.center, 0.02, { state: "violated" }));
  },
  { label: "finite subfamily + a point it misses" },
);
lesson.show(subfamily);
lesson.say`Keep only ${finiteBudget} of the boxes. Each freed slot lets us choose the coordinate that box forbids, so we can assemble a point ${escapes} lying outside every kept box. No finite subfamily covers the product.`;

// ── Beat 6 ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
lesson.beat("Compactness fails by fineness");
const conclusion = F.tex`${F.span("space", "\\{0,1\\}^{\\mathbb{N}}")} \text{ compact per factor, yet } \textbf{not compact in the box topology}`;
lesson.show(View.formula(conclusion));
lesson.say`Even though every factor \\{0,1\\} is compact, ${conclusion} fails to be compact: the box opens are so fine that each pins infinitely many coordinates, and finiteness can never reach every point. Fineness, not the factors, is what breaks compactness.`;

// ── What the student can DO ────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Drag the budget: no matter how many boxes you keep, the escaping point stays,
// and the claim never flips — that IS the failure of compactness.
lesson.expose(finiteBudget, { as: "sliders" });
lesson.expose(topology, { as: "sliders" });
lesson.say`Try it: drag ${finiteBudget} up to keep more boxes — ${escapes} still holds. Then pull ${topology} back toward the product topology, where only finitely many slots are pinned and compactness returns.`;
```
